# {{.Module}}

Minimal Palma application. Requires Go 1.26 or newer.

```sh
go mod tidy
go tool pfw run -env {{.Environment}}
```

To generate wiring and build separately:

```sh
go generate ./internal/bootstrap
go test ./...
go build -o bin/app ./cmd/app
PFW_ENV={{.Environment}} ./bin/app
```

The CLI is pinned through the `tool` directive in go.mod. With a local framework
replacement, the checkout must remain available; remove the replacement and pin
a published framework version to share the project independently.

`compose.go` declares supported environments and uses `pfw.Discover("../...")`
to discover providers in `internal`. Mark new dependency constructors with
`//pfw:coconut`, as shown by `application.NewApp`; no constructor list is needed.
`pfw.AutoBind()` is enabled at the build root: a single compatible provider
is selected automatically for an interface. Use `pfw.Bind` when there are several
compatible implementations. Modules inherit the root setting unless they declare
`pfw.AutoBind()` or `pfw.AutoBind(false)`. Add your own names to `pfw.Environments(...)`. Generate again for each selected environment
before building; the binary rejects an environment different from its wiring.

`pfw.toml` configures the bootstrap and main package paths, relative to the module
root. `go tool pfw generate`, `inspect` and `run` use these paths when no package
is supplied; explicit package arguments override them.
